## Step-by-Step Safe Verification Framework
To test these parameters safely without violating the 5 requests per second limit or causing service disruptions, use specialized, passive webhooks.
## Step 1: Set Up an External Listener (Non-Destructive Callback)
Never use internal cloud metadata addresses (like 169.254.169.254) or private local networks (127.0.0.1) during initial checks to prevent system disruptions. instead, utilize a unique interaction domain from a tool like Interactsh or Burp Collaborator.
## Step 2: Injecting the Safe Payload
Replace the value of target parameters with your collaborator URL.

* Standard Injection: ?visit_domain=yourinteractshdomain.com
* URL/Protocol Injection: &lobbyUrl=http://yourinteractshdomain.com
* Encoded Injection: If modifying redirect_data, modify the JSON value inside the string, re-encode it into standard base64 format, and swap it into your request body or URL query.

## Step 3: Differentiating between Client and Server Interactions

* Client Interaction (Open Redirect / Normal Behavior): If your browser changes location to your listener domain, or if your listener registers an IP address belonging to your own local router/ISP, the application is handling the redirect entirely client-side. This is typically out-of-scope or a low-severity open redirect.
* Server Interaction (SSRF Confirmation): If your listener receives an incoming HTTP request or DNS query originating from an external IP address belonging to the 1win infrastructure (e.g., AWS, Cloudflare, or their dedicated hosting provider), the backend server initiated the connection.

